 succeeded in 0ms:
   112	| **New code** | `scripts/loop.py` (CLI), `loop_tasks.py` (store + lock), `loop_runner.py` (outer loop, backoff, caps, verify, git), `loop_notify.py` (secrets + Telegram), `loop_log.py` (logging + redaction). Root wrappers `loop` (sh) and `loop.cmd`. `run-loop.sh` / `run-loop.ps1` stay as entry points but become ~10-line wrappers around `loop run`. |
   113	| **Reuse** | `council_state.py` config loading/validation, history, `run-summary`; `discover_tests.py` for default checks; `council_doctor.py`; `/council-cycle` unchanged in structure; all six agents. `/goal`'s archive-and-reset steps move into a `council_state.py set-goal` subcommand so `/goal` and the runner share one implementation. |
   114	| **Task store** | `tasks/tasks.json` in the target project (path configurable). Fields: `id`, `description`, `acceptance` (text for Arbiter/Realist), `verify` (list of shell commands; exit 0 = pass), `status` (`pending / claimed / done / failed / blocked`), `owner`, `attempts`, `depends_on`, `branch`, `commit`, `reason`, `notes[]`, `test_changes[]`, timestamps. Untracked by default (`loop init` adds it to `.git/info/exclude`): a tracked file would dirty the tree on every status change and conflict across task branches. |
   115	| **Lock** | `tasks/tasks.lock`, created with `O_CREAT\|O_EXCL` (atomic on NTFS and POSIX), holding pid + host + time. Held for milliseconds around read-modify-write. Waiters retry with jitter (10 s limit, then a clear error). Stale = holder PID dead on the same host, else older than 30 s. Reclaim is by atomic rename, then re-check, which closes race F7. Writes go to a temp file, `fsync`, then `os.replace` (atomic on both OSes; retried on Windows sharing errors). Same fix applied to `cycle.lock` (F6, F7). |
   116	| **Run lock** | `.council/state/run.lock` with the runner's real PID. A second `loop run` on the same project exits with a clear message (F8). Several workers are still safe at the store level (each needs its own git worktree) — the parallel launcher itself is out of scope (§9). |
   117	| **Verification gate** | Run by the runner, never by the model. Commands come from the task's `verify`, else `config.test_commands`, else `discover_tests.py`. **No runnable check → the task cannot become `done`; it goes `blocked` ("no runnable check").** Each command has a timeout; output tail is stored in `reason`. |
   118	| **No test gaming** | Before the gate, the runner diffs the task branch against base for test files. Any deleted or modified (not newly added) test needs a one-line reason logged with `loop note <id> --test-change "<why>"`, stored in `test_changes[]`; otherwise the gate fails. A deleted test file always goes `blocked` for review. During this revamp Fable reviews every such entry. |
   119	| **Auto-resume** | All state is on disk. On `loop run` / `loop resume` start: any `claimed` task whose owner is not a live run goes back to `pending` (attempts kept); leftover uncommitted work on its branch is saved with `git stash push -u` (named, never discarded) so the dirty-tree stop no longer needs a human (F3). |
   120	| **Outer loop** | `claude -p "/council-loop:council-cycle" --plugin-dir <plugin> --output-format json`, with a per-session timeout. Exit code and JSON are always checked. Usage-limit / rate-limit / overload / API errors and non-zero exits → exponential backoff: 60 s × 2ⁿ, capped at 1 h, with jitter; a "resets at …" time is used when it can be parsed. These failures do **not** count as task attempts. |
   121	| **Caps** | `max_attempts` 3 per task, `max_sessions` 50 per run, `max_hours` 12 per run (backoff sleeps count). Hitting any one: stop, write the reason to the log and `loop status`, send one Telegram message, exit non-zero. All three in config. The existing per-goal ceiling (`max_cycles` / `max_minutes`) stays and applies per attempt. |
   122	| **Blocked** | New council outcome: the Arbiter may return `BLOCKED: <reason>`; the cycle writes `stop.flag` = `blocked: <reason>`. `council-cycle.md` gets one rule: when headless, never ask a question — block instead. Runner maps it to status `blocked`. |
   123	| **Escalation** | Night Shift's channel is the Telegram Bot API from one bot to the owner's chat (`control/bot.py`, config names `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN` + `TELEGRAM_OWNER_ID`). The loop makes a **send-only** `sendMessage` call with that bot's token, fetched through the secrets backend. Send-only does not disturb Night Shift's long-poll. Night Shift itself is not changed or contacted. Sent only for: task blocked (credentials, ambiguity, destructive action needed), a cap hit, run finished with failures. One message per event (`escalated_at` stored). Replies are not read: the owner answers with `loop resume`. If the token is unavailable: write `logs/escalations.md`, warn in `loop status`, keep running. |
   124	| **CLI** | `loop init` · `loop add "desc" [--accept … --verify … --depends … --id …]` · `loop run [--max-sessions --max-hours --once]` · `loop status [--json]` · `loop resume [--retry-failed] [--unblock <id>]` · `loop logs [-n] [--task] [--follow]` · `loop note <id> …`. `argparse`, so every command has `--help`. Every command is safe to repeat. |
   125	| **Logs** | `logs/loop.jsonl`: one JSON object per event with `ts`, `run_id`, `task_id`, `agent`, `model`, `event`, `outcome`, `duration_s`, `cost_usd`, `tokens`. Role-level rows are derived from each new `history.jsonl` record plus `config.models`. Rotation: stdlib `RotatingFileHandler`, 5 MB × 5. Raw session output goes to `logs/sessions/`, newest 200 kept. A redaction filter strips token-shaped strings before anything is written; URLs containing a token are never logged. |
   126	| **Secrets** | `secrets.backend` in config: `infisical` (default — shells out to the `infisical` CLI read-only, using whatever login that machine already has), `env`, or `command` (a template such as the owner's `secret` tool). **Never writes to Infisical, never reads or edits its config.** Values stay in memory only. Missing CLI → clear message, escalation degrades to the file log. |
   127	| **Deny-list hook** | One Python hook, `scripts/hooks/guard.py` (no `jq`, same behaviour on both OSes), holding the two existing rules plus a new `Bash` matcher. Denies: `rm -rf` and variants (`Remove-Item -Recurse -Force`, `rd /s`); `git push --force / -f / --force-with-lease / --mirror`; any push whose target is `main`/`master` (incl. `HEAD:main`, bare `git push` while on main); `git merge / rebase / cherry-pick / commit` while on `main`/`master`; disk tools (`mkfs*`, `fdisk`, `parted`, `sfdisk`, `sgdisk`, `wipefs`, `dd of=/dev/…`, `diskpart`, `format X:`, `Format-Volume`, `Clear-Disk`); reading or writing credential files (`.env`, `*.pem`, `id_rsa*`, `id_ed25519*`, `.credentials.json`, `.netrc`, `.git-credentials`, `.aws/credentials`, Infisical config); `infisical secrets set/delete`. Fails **closed** on a payload it cannot parse. Active in every loop-run session (the runner sets `COUNCIL_LOOP_RUN=1`) and, via a project `.claude/settings.json`, in every session opened in this repo. A regex deny-list can be bypassed; the real boundary is GitHub branch protection (§6). |
   128	| **Config** | A new `loop` block in the existing `.council/config.json` (+ schema + example + validation): caps, backoff, session timeout, `base_branch`, `branch_prefix`, `tasks_file`, `logs_dir`, log sizes, `claude_bin`, `secrets`, `notify`. All have defaults; paths are relative to the project; nothing hard-coded. |
   129	| **Git hygiene** | `loop init` records the current branch as `base_branch` and refuses `main`/`master`. Each task gets `task/<id>-<slug>` off base. The council commits per accepted cycle on that branch (unchanged). After the gate passes: `git merge --no-ff` into base — one merge commit per verified task. Failed or blocked task branches are left in place for inspection. The runner never pushes. For this revamp: base = `revamp/console-loop`. |
